#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
# infra/core-deploy — installed by the governor as /usr/local/sbin/core-deploy (root:root 0755).
"""
core-deploy — make a released commit the code CORE runs (ADR-132 D10 · D2).

Usage (run by the governor, as root):

    sudo core-deploy <tag-or-sha> [--migrate] [--merged-by NAME]
    sudo core-deploy --rollback

Run by the governor, or by the post-merge runner (core-pipeline, as
core-runner, through a sudoers rule naming this script only).

Deployment is the act that makes code run with production authority
(ADR-132 D10.8): the assistant may change, build and test source; only this
script, run by the governor, moves a commit into the runtime.

Who executes what:

    git (fetch, rev-parse, archive, show)  core        CORE's own clone, D1's safe git options
    wheel build, dependency download       core-build  build backends execute code; never root or core
    venv install                           root        wheels only, hash-checked; installing runs no package code
    migrations, readiness, status          core        the services' own account and environment
                                                       (systemd-run in core's user manager)

Integrity: dependency hashes come from ``poetry.lock`` bytes read through
core's git, never from the build tree; the CORE wheel core-build produced is
accepted only if every file in it is byte-identical to ``src/<path>`` in the
archive, its console scripts equal pyproject's, and it carries no ``.pth``.

Order (ADR-132 D10 · D2):
 1. resolve the ref to a SHA once; refuse unless it is on the upstream deploy
    branch (``DEPLOY_BRANCH`` in deploy.conf; develop when unset, main for
    the one-path flow) and its CI check runs are all green;
 2. refuse if releases/<sha> exists;
 3. build (core-build) and install (root) into releases/<sha>/venv, then
    write core-release.json {sha, wheel_sha256} (the C4 contract);
 3a. law: governed text that changed between the live commit and <sha>
    (``.intent/`` except custody, ``.specs/``, ``.claude/`` settings and
    hooks) is applied by core-law running the NEW release's
    ``core-admin law apply-merge`` (no PyPI release involved): the merge on
    GitHub is the approval, recorded in core-law's signed ledger. A refusal
    stops the deploy before anything switched. If the deploy later switches
    back, the law is put back the same way;
 4. migration check with the new release (read-only); pending migrations
    without --migrate refuse;
 5. --migrate: stop every unit, back up (BACKUP_COMMAND), migrate as core.
    Any failure from here on with a migrated schema stops and hands the
    governor the choice (exit 3) — the old release is never restarted
    against a schema it does not expect;
 6. readiness: the new release's API on a spare loopback port must answer
    /health, which it does only after its law-root and schema gates pass;
 7. switch ``current``, restart every unit, require all active and every
    worker that was alive before to heartbeat again; without a migration a
    failure switches back;
 8. self-update: the installed core-deploy, core-pipeline and
    core-sandbox-check become the deployed commit's ``infra/`` copies;
 9. append a ledger line.

A merge that changes the host setup (``infra/systemd/``, the setup scripts:
sudoers, accounts, units) is refused before anything is built, with the exact
commands for the governor (``--host-setup-applied`` after applying it).

``--rollback`` returns to the release the current one replaced, refusing
when a migration lies between them (a symlink does not roll back a database).

Exit codes: 0 done; 1 refused (nothing live changed, or switched back);
3 stopped after a migration — governor decision needed.

The heartbeat check reads GET /v1/daemon/status over TCP. Since
``require_governor`` went live (ADR-132 D10.11 step 4) that route stays open
as a read; daemon start and stop need the governor.
"""

